ive CM ... - https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Glavin2020%20Asuka1.pdf\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.78\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.73,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.85,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the best-supported complete sequence in the submitted network. Ritson and Sutherland (2013) support glycolaldehyde production, and Foden et al. (2020) support its Strecker conversion to serine aminonitrile. However, the reported 42% glycolaldehyde yield is not a serine yield. The principal unresolved steps are hydrolysis to free serine under specified surface conditions and control of irradiation before glycolaldehyde is over-reduced. The S8 endpoint is balanced but remains a sulfur-speciation model.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify UV spectrum, exposure time, concentrations, pH, buffer and copper speciation, and quantify competition with rxn_018.\",\n        \"Provide a physically justified transition from photoreduction to Strecker chemistry, including termination of irradiation and ammonia addition.\",\n        \"Establish hydrolysis conditions and rates for both serine aminonitrile and serinamide, measuring free-serine recovery and degradation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.78\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.93,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.84,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n      \"feedback\": \"Copper-free photoreduction is experimentally supported by the supplied account of Ritson and Sutherland (2013); copper omission is not a chemical defect. Relative to P1, the supplied evidence provides less quantitative constraint on the operating window and product selectivity of this regime. The downstream Strecker sequence is sound, but mild-condition hydrolysis and net serine recovery remain unresolved.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Use copper-free-specific irradiation and yield data rather than transferring the copper-assisted 42% glycolald