conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.86,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"The strongest reference pathway in the supplied evidence. Ritson and Sutherland (2013) support glycolaldehyde production, and Foden et al. (2020) support its Strecker conversion to serine aminonitrile. Acid hydrolysis is chemically conventional, but its operating window and recovered serine yield are not specified here. Physical separation is sensible, although transfer recovery and stopping photochemistry before rxn_021 consumes glycolaldehyde remain unvalidated.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify hydrolysis acidity, temperature and residence time, and measure aminonitrile, serinamide and free serine throughout.\",\n        \"Demonstrate irradiation termination and transfer into Strecker conditions with measured glycolaldehyde recovery and cyanohydrin competition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.83\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.9,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.81,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"The upstream chemistry is well supported, and alkaline nitrile/amide hydrolysis provides a legitimate route to serine. The unresolved issue is whether hydrolysis proceeds sufficiently rapidly without excessive substrate or product degradation under the chosen alkaline conditions. No reaction-specific operating parameters or comparative acid-versus-base recovery data are supplied. The neutral-parent representation of aqueous serinate is acceptable and is not a conservation or target-identity problem.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure alkaline hydrolysis and serine-loss kinetics together rather than inferring recovery from aminonitrile disappearance.\",\n        \"Document base supply, residence time, subsequent conditioning and transfer losses.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.91,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.83,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Copper-free cyanosulfidic reduction is supported by the supplied literature and is not intrinsically defective. Its slower reported behavior makes the productive irradiation window important, particularly because glycolaldehyde can undergo further reduction. The copper-assisted 42% glycolaldehyde result cannot be assigned to this branch without measurement. Subsequent Strecker chemistry and acid hydrolysis are plausible, but complete-route recovery remains unestablished.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure copper-free conversion and glycolaldehyde selectivity against photon dose under the proposed water chemistry.\",\n        \"Establish an end-to-end mass balance through transfer, Strecker conversion and acid hydrolysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.83\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.89,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.68,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.79,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a chemically credible operational branch, with uncertainty concentrated in copper-free photoreduction selectivity and alkaline recovery of serine. Neither copper omission nor the use of base is itself a defect. The concern is the absence of demonstrated operating windows showing that glycolaldehyde formation outpaces rxn_021 and that subsequent hydrolysis outpaces serine loss.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Optimize photochemical residence time using measured glycolaldehyde and acetaldehyde concentrations.\",\n        \"Test the alkaline hydrolysis branch on the actual transferred reaction mixture, including its residual sulfur and cyanide species.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.65,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.79,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n      \"feedback\": \"Xu et al. (2018) support cyanosulfitic formation of reduced products including the glycolaldehyde bisulfite adduct, not this complete route to recovered serine. Adduct dissociation is chemically reasonable but does not guarantee an adequate free-aldehyde concentration. The submission does not specify whether ferrocyanide assistance is intended. Residual sulfite, adduct re-formation and competing glyceronitrile production require quantitative treatment. The balanced dithionate endpoint remains provisional rather than experimentally established.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the photosensitizer, sulfur speciation and irradiation conditions; distinguish the reported adduct fraction from total reduced-product yield.\",\n        \"Measure adduct dissociation and Strecker capture together, including sulfite carryover and losses during conditioning.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.96,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.83\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.63,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.75,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n      \"feedback\": \"The proposed transformations are chemically recognizable, but two important interfaces lack validation: release and capture of bisulfite-bound glycolaldehyde, and alkaline conversion of the resulting aminonitrile into surviving free serine. The supplied cyanosulfitic evidence establishes an upstream component rather than this integrated sequence. Separate zones avoid assuming simultaneous compatibility, but their chemical conditioning and material recovery are unspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n      