ievement\": 0.74,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"Alkaline hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile through glycinamide is chemically sound. However, a basic hydrolysate is not automatically suitable for selective copper-mediated hydroxymethylation: pH, residual cyanide, ammonia and copper complexation must be controlled. As in P7, the final reaction has synthetic precedent, but active Cu(II) availability, product distribution and free-serine recovery have not been established for this environmental implementation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Measure residual cyanide and characterize copper speciation after mixing the glycine hydrolysate with the hydroxymethylation zone.\",\n        \"Establish formaldehyde dosing and residence time that limit over-hydroxymethylation while preserving recoverable serine.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.91,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.56,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.75,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) provide direct support for Ni/H2 conversion of glyoxylate to glycine. Their omission of serine is not evidence against rxn_016, since this network makes serine by a subsequent, different reaction. The 'Biochemical' label does not specify a physical reaction setting. Nickel catalyst form, hydrogen pressure and the transition from reducing Ni/H2 conditions to active Cu(II) chemistry require explicit implementation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\",\n        \"rxn_020\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the nonenzymatic aqueous environment and distinguish the published nickel catalyst and pre